# Tokens for `url_lexical`. The input is
#   u32 port | query \0 url
# so the NUL that ends the query field is itself a token worth having:
# without it the fuzzer spends its whole budget inside one giant query and
# never reaches `parse` at all.
"\x00"

# --- x-www-form-urlencoded, the differential's grammar ----------------
# The three separators, plus the `+`-as-space and `%XX` rules that are the
# entire difference between `form_decode` and a plain percent-decode.
"&"
"="
"+"
"?"
"?a=1&b=2"
"a=1&a=2"
"&&"
"=v"
"flag"
"%20"
"%2B"
"%3D"
"%26"
"%00"
"%C3%B1"
# Malformed escapes: a truncated one, a non-hex one, a lone `%`. All three
# must survive `form_decode` and must survive it the same way on both
# sides of the differential.
"%2"
"%zz"
"%"
# Lower-case hex: percent-encoding emits upper case, so this is the input
# that tells a case-folding decoder from an exact one.
"%c3%b1"

# --- RFC 3986, the splitter's grammar ---------------------------------
"://"
"//"
"https://"
"mailto:"
"MailTo:"
"@"
"#"
# Authority shapes: userinfo, the IPv6 brackets `split_authority` special
# cases, and the port edges either side of the u16 the parse lands in.
"user@"
"a@b@"
"[::1]"
"[::1]:3000"
":8443"
":0"
":65535"
":65536"
":99999"
":abc"
"?#"
"//?#"
